WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.280
This video was made possible by CuriosityStream.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.950
Watch an exclusive companion video to this
by signing up for the CuriosityStream/Nebula

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:11.740
bundle deal at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.699
Ten years ago, in May 2011, Adele’s Rolling
in the Deep was topping the Billboard charts,

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:21.599
the first season of Games of Thrones was on
its way to becoming a television phenomenon,

00:00:21.599 --> 00:00:26.239
Osama Bin Laden had just been killed by US
Navy Seals, and the International Olympic

00:00:26.239 --> 00:00:30.619
Committee was sending letters to National
Olympic Committees soliciting bids to host

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:34.590
the 32nd running of the Summer Olympic Games.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.820
This moment marked the start of a decade long
process of planning and preparation all leading

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.800
up to a mere 16 days of international sporting
competition.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.500
Step two was this: one month later, the governor
of Tokyo, Japan announced his city’s intention

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:56.230
to submit a bid—in large part to mark their
recovery from the massive, 20,000 death earthquake

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.120
that had devastated the country mere months
before.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:06.030
Soon enough, Madrid, Istanbul, Baku, and Doha
each submitted bids as well but, in the end,

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:11.190
after a two year evaluation process, a contract
was signed between the International Olympic

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:16.990
Committee, the Japanese Olympic committee,
and the City of Tokyo in September, 2013—Tokyo

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:21.550
would host the 2020 Summer Olympic Games.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:26.510
For an event of this scale—tens of thousands
of athletes, millions of spectators, and billions

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.950
of television viewers—a mere seven year
planning period, believe it or not, leaves

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.110
no time to waste.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:37.800
The most watched television broadcast in existence
needs to be planned; a whole village needs

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.200
to be constructed; dozens of stadiums and
venues need to be built, refurbished, or adapted;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.310
and one of the world’s largest cities needs
to plan for what might be the busiest three

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.460
weeks of its existence.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:56.120
To complete this task, Tokyo was given this:
the Host City Contract - Operational Requirements

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.130
document.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:03.650
Across its 273 pages, this outlines, in incredible
detail, what the International Olympic Committee,

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:09.530
or IOC, requires from its contractual partner,
Tokyo, in order complete the Olympics successfully

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.760
and, crucially, consistently with all past
and future Games.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.480
Its essentially the bible for Olympic organizers.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:24.280
The 2015 edition, made for the 2020 Games—now
being held in 2021—outlines some absolutely

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.280
minuscule details.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.000
For example, Tokyo had to install weather
stations at the sites of all outdoor competition

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.150
venues three years prior to the start of the
Games in order to collect long-term data to

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.860
be used by the IOC to inform their scheduling
of events.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.030
The document also establishes some basic but
far reaching guidelines, such as the fact

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.709
that the Olympics will last 16 days, except
for a certain number of preliminary competitions

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.670
that, subject to IOC approval, can be held
prior to the opening ceremony.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:57.520
It also, in a matter of mere paragraphs, outlines
massive asks, such as the necessity for Tokyo

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.209
to book 41,177 hotel rooms for the media,
marketing partners, future host city representatives,

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:08.390
IOC executives, National Olympic Committee
representatives, International Sport Federation

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.250
representatives, and more.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:15.349
This is equivalent to close to half of all
of Tokyo’s hotel rooms, and it only represents

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:20.319
the accommodation requirements for those actually
running the games—not those competing or

00:03:20.319 --> 00:03:21.319
spectating.

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:24.920
The hotel capacity crunch was such an issue,
in fact, that prior to the prohibition of

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.190
international spectators due to the Coronavirus
pandemic, the city had planned to dock cruise

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:33.440
ships in Tokyo Bay to serve as additional,
temporary hotels.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.209
Athletes, however, do not stay in hotels.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:42.569
Since the 1932 Los Angeles Games, each host
city constructs a dedicated Olympics Village

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:47.129
to house athletes—far away from the crowds
of fans and hoards of media.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:52.020
Now, the IOC’s operational requirements
set strict standards to assure that, for the

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:58.130
most part, the 2012 village is just like the
2020 village, for all practical purposes.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:03.670
For example, it has to be within 50 kilometers,
31 miles, or a 60 minute drive of all venues

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:08.409
so, if any event takes place further from
the main host city, an additional village

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:09.680
is constructed.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.750
This is especially relevant for the Winter
Games, with which indoor sports often happen

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.510
within the main host city, but outdoor events
happen further away at mountain resorts.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:23.300
For example, in the case of the 2014 games,
a primary village was constructed in Sochi,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.520
while an additional one was set up at the
Rosa Khutor ski resort where snowsports events

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.520
took place.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.411
For the summer games in Tokyo, however, just
one village was constructed, and the Operational

00:04:33.411 --> 00:04:38.669
Requirements dictated that it must have the
capacity for 16,000 people, so it operates

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:40.930
at the scale of a small town.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:46.140
The requirements also dictate that rooms must
be at least 97 square feet or 9 square meters

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:51.810
in size, or 129 square feet or 12 square meters
with double rooms, and even outline their

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.810
required contents.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:59.060
In addition, the village must have an Olympics
merchandise store, a bank, photo store, hair

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:03.520
salon, florist, dry cleaning shop, travel
agent, post office, and more.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.370
Typically, McDonalds also sets up a temporary
restaurant in the village for the games, which

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.919
is like any McDonalds except that its menu
has no prices—the company provides athletes

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:17.090
with food for free in exchange for the publicity
it inevitably attracts.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:22.530
Lastly, according to the operational requirements
document, the village even must have a designated

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.310
place of mourning.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:29.590
Now, constructing brand new dedicated venues,
such as the village, for the purposes of a

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.670
16-day event quite obviously presents problems.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:37.450
While in decades past cities were essentially
willing to throw any amount of money at the

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:42.030
Games—and sometimes run at hundreds of percents
over budget—due to a belief that the publicity

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:46.790
they attracted was invaluable, taxpayers,
and therefore governments, are increasingly

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:51.440
skeptical of the value that the Games truly
provides for a city and country.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.580
There are plenty of horror stories of places
like Turin, Italy, the host of the 2006 Games,

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:01.259
whose village now sits derelict and is inhabited
by squatters—essentially a complete waste

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:05.710
of money from the perspective of the city
and, therefore, its taxpayers.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:10.190
There are also, however, cases like that of
Sydney, which redeveloped its Olympic Park

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.500
into a true neighborhood.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:18.039
It’s now home to upscale hotels, restaurants,
and housing complexes, and hosts nearly 6,000

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:21.100
events both large and small each year.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:25.889
Altogether, having seen the horror stories,
the Olympics are becoming less attractive

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:27.139
for cities.

00:06:27.139 --> 00:06:31.790
In decades past, plenty of cities would bid
to host each Olympics, but now there are situations

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:37.080
like Paris 2024 or Los Angeles 2028 where
cities are awarded the Games effectively by

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:41.130
default—having faced zero competition in
the bidding process.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:46.050
For those that do host the games, though,
cost-consciousness has increased dramatically

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:50.740
and they’re now trying to craft their venue
strategy closer to that of Sydney, rather

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.039
than Turin.

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:56.600
In Tokyo, three categories of Olympic venues
are being used.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.680
The first is temporary.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.240
With certain events, such as canoeing, climbing,
beach volleyball, or sailing, there’s little

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.680
prospect that the city can redevelop the venue
into something self-sustaining after the games,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.580
so they either set up spectator and media
facilities at existing sporting facilities

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:16.630
that just lack the scale to host an Olympic
event, or they set up an entirely new, temporary

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:17.630
venue.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:21.259
For more popular sports that require less
specialized facilities, however, Tokyo is

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:24.199
using pre-existing venues around the city.

00:07:24.199 --> 00:07:28.860
For example, with Table Tennis, which requires
little infrastructure, the Tokyo Metropolitan

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:35.100
Gymnasium is being used—just as it was during
the 1964 Tokyo Olympics for Gymnastics.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:40.229
With certain events, however, the Tokyo organizers
believed it was actually worth it to build

00:07:40.229 --> 00:07:42.440
a brand new venue.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:47.229
Swimming and diving events, for example, will
happen at the brand-new Olympics Aquatics

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:48.229
Center.

00:07:48.229 --> 00:07:50.710
This is often the case at summer games.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:54.789
Swimming and diving are typically some of
the most watched sports at the Olympics, despite

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:58.400
mediocre popularity as a spectator sport outside
of the games.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.050
That means cities often lack aquatics venues
with the scale necessary for Olympic events,

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.970
so this is one of the venues more often built
as new.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:11.790
At the 2016 Rio de Janeiro games, a temporary
aquatics stadium was built, while at London

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:18.550
2012, Beijing 2008, Sydney 2000, and plenty
more, new, permanent facilities were created.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:23.920
Now, with each of these new, permanent venues,
the Bureau of Olympic and Paralympic Games

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:30.410
Tokyo 2020 Preparation had to justify their
long-term value beyond the games to the government

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:31.410
and public.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.930
Therefore, they released this document.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:39.319
It outlines the core of their development
strategy: they want to create not only effective

00:08:39.319 --> 00:08:43.940
venues, but an appealing neighborhood, certainly
following the model of Sydney 2000, so that

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:48.230
they can consider any money spent an investment,
rather than a cost.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:52.610
With each of these new venues, such as the
new Olympic Aquatics Centre, for example,

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:56.180
they’re published a detailed post-games
plan.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.230
Through redeveloping the venue into both a
long-term competition venue and a community

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:04.899
recreation center, the Bureau believes they
can attract a million visitors per year after

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:08.959
the games, and while they project the facility
to run at a loss, its one small enough to

00:09:08.959 --> 00:09:13.670
be comparable with other similar public recreation
centers created outside of the context of

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:14.879
the Olympics.

00:09:14.879 --> 00:09:19.519
Some of the other venues, such as the Ariake
Arena, to be used for Volleyball, are expected

00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:25.899
to turn a profit after the games so, altogether,
if projections are accurate, these new venues

00:09:25.899 --> 00:09:30.240
won’t be a long-term financial burden to
the city and its taxpayers.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:36.189
Now, of course, like with anything regarding
the Olympics, each of the 42 venues need to

00:09:36.189 --> 00:09:39.129
follow a sprawling set of strict standards.

00:09:39.129 --> 00:09:43.889
For example, they each need to have the Olympics
flag and respective International Sporting

00:09:43.889 --> 00:09:47.339
Federation flag flying in a conspicuous spot.

00:09:47.339 --> 00:09:52.079
They need to have a full backup power system
for all broadcast, media, technology, sport,

00:09:52.079 --> 00:09:56.809
and security operations, while their lighting
systems must be split into two sections with

00:09:56.809 --> 00:10:01.879
independent power supplies for each so that
events can go on even through power failure.

00:10:01.879 --> 00:10:06.889
In terms of the actual sporting facilities,
however, these are each subject to the standards

00:10:06.889 --> 00:10:11.040
of the respective IOC-recognized International
Sports Federation.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.230
For example, Olympic rowing is managed by
the International Rowing Federation, which

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:22.739
recommends 531 feet or 162 meters of course
width, and requires 2,162 meters or 7,126

00:10:22.739 --> 00:10:24.899
feet of length.

00:10:24.899 --> 00:10:29.160
Their standards get plenty more specific,
though: they specify the intricacies of the

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:34.749
course buoy system; outline where advertising
banners can go; detail the location, construction,

00:10:34.749 --> 00:10:38.790
and operation of timing huts; and much, much
more.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:43.799
Each Olympic event is essentially the pinnacle
of its sport, so the International Federations

00:10:43.799 --> 00:10:46.059
are heavily involved.

00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:50.519
Of course, the Olympics are a made-for-television
event.

00:10:50.519 --> 00:10:55.410
While millions of spectators do typically
watch the Games in person, billions watch

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.759
on TV.

00:10:56.759 --> 00:11:02.079
In fact, 73% of the Olympics total revenue
comes from television rights alone.

00:11:02.079 --> 00:11:07.670
They simply would not happen in their modern
form if not for their massive television audience

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:14.360
so perhaps the most important facility of
all is this: the International Broadcast Center.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.220
In Tokyo, this facility is constructed in
Tokyo Big Sight—the country’s largest

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:22.779
convention center—and is run by the Olympics
Broadcasting Services, or OBS.

00:11:22.779 --> 00:11:27.450
Now, for the longest time, broadcasting operations
were something that the International Olympic

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:32.379
Committee would contract out to a third party,
but in advance of the 2008 Beijing Olympics,

00:11:32.379 --> 00:11:37.189
OBS was established to act as the host broadcaster
for all future Games.

00:11:37.189 --> 00:11:43.600
Now, like with most large sporting events,
you won’t see cameras from NBC, BBC, Eurosport,

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.980
and all the other national broadcasters that
carry the events on TV actually at each event.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:53.999
That’s because OBS is responsible for filming
and producing each event, and then they provide

00:11:53.999 --> 00:11:57.620
produced feeds to each broadcaster, who acts
as their customer.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:03.209
While NBC, for example, might have reporters
and commenters at each event to augment the

00:12:03.209 --> 00:12:07.089
OBS feeds, they don’t actually film the
sporting events themselves.

00:12:07.089 --> 00:12:14.449
Now, OBS is a standalone, independent company
set up by the IOC to fulfill these broadcast

00:12:14.449 --> 00:12:15.629
functions.

00:12:15.629 --> 00:12:22.439
While typically it has some 166 employees,
this balloons up to 8,100 during the Games—mostly

00:12:22.439 --> 00:12:26.880
comprised of the operational staff filming
and producing the television feeds.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:33.439
Its ultimate task in Tokyo is to create 9,500
hours of content—more than with any prior

00:12:33.439 --> 00:12:35.040
Olympic Games.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:42.120
To accomplish this, it has deployed some 1,049
cameras and 3,600 microphones across Tokyo’s

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.689
42 venues.

00:12:43.689 --> 00:12:47.619
These are cut together into produced “World
Feeds” in dedicated broadcast compounds

00:12:47.619 --> 00:12:52.859
at each venue before being delivered to here:
the International Broadcast Center.

00:12:52.859 --> 00:12:58.439
From one month before the games until its
conclusion, this is a 24/7 operation and,

00:12:58.439 --> 00:13:03.299
much like the Olympics Village, operates like
a small town with restaurants, coffee shops,

00:13:03.299 --> 00:13:08.939
bars, a medical center, bank, convenience
store, dry cleaners, and even a massage room.

00:13:08.939 --> 00:13:13.670
Many of OBS’ facilities at the International
Broadcast Center deal with the technical aspects

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:18.249
of taking the World Feeds, of which as many
as 64 are produced simultaneously in the case

00:13:18.249 --> 00:13:22.550
of day two of the games, and disseminating
these out to all the different rights holding

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:24.779
broadcasters around the world.

00:13:24.779 --> 00:13:29.989
However, in addition to television production,
much of the purpose of this facility is to

00:13:29.989 --> 00:13:34.589
act as workspace for all the journalists from
around the world who have travelled to the

00:13:34.589 --> 00:13:35.589
Games.

00:13:35.589 --> 00:13:40.279
During the Olympics, one of OBS’ main functions
is essentially as a landlord.

00:13:40.279 --> 00:13:44.889
They manage the International Broadcast Center
and rent out space to different broadcasters

00:13:44.889 --> 00:13:47.290
to serve as their workspaces.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:52.999
A private office in the center comes in units
of 270 square feet or 25 square meters, and

00:13:52.999 --> 00:13:58.699
in the case of Tokyo, costs some $5,011 per
unit for the duration of the games.

00:13:58.699 --> 00:14:02.649
Of course, though, there are plenty of upcharges.

00:14:02.649 --> 00:14:07.079
Even once a broadcaster has paid to rent a
space, two normal wall plugs will cost them

00:14:07.079 --> 00:14:14.089
$876, and the OBS rate card even includes
the option to purchase a power strip for $27.

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:19.470
Meanwhile, getting a dedicated 100 Mbps ethernet
plug, to provide more stable internet than

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:23.689
the free wifi, costs broadcasters $19,044.

00:14:23.689 --> 00:14:29.809
OBS also sells broadcasters a digital data
feed, giving real-time access to results,

00:14:29.809 --> 00:14:33.910
schedules, medals, records, news, biographies,
statistics, and more.

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:38.420
This is what ultimately gets repackaged into,
for example, the real-time results you’ll

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.040
see on the NBC or the BBC websites.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:45.629
Finally, OBS’ rate card includes options
to outfit a broadcaster’s private office

00:14:45.629 --> 00:14:51.019
with a printer for $356, a walkie talkie for
$438, small folding table for $67, microwave

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:55.129
for $105, and electric kettle for $23.

00:14:55.129 --> 00:15:00.050
With 6,000 accredited journalists awarded
access to the Tokyo Games, its as much a job

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:04.939
to manage them as the athletes and, while
procuring an electric kettle for an LA Times

00:15:04.939 --> 00:15:09.939
sports writer might seem like a trivial task,
it makes the journalists’ jobs easier and

00:15:09.939 --> 00:15:14.910
doing so plays a tiny but present part in
helping the Olympics run smoothly.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:20.519
Now, ultimately, while there are plenty of
big decisions required in planning the Olympics,

00:15:20.519 --> 00:15:21.879
there are few small ones.

00:15:21.879 --> 00:15:26.709
For the most part, the host cities’ jobs
are just to follow an incredibly detailed

00:15:26.709 --> 00:15:28.540
set of instructions.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:33.279
The spend the better part of a decade doing
exactly that, but the ultimate goal of the

00:15:33.279 --> 00:15:38.389
2020 Games is to make an event that’s just
as successful and runs largely the same as

00:15:38.389 --> 00:15:43.999
the Games of 2016, 2012, 2008, or any year
before.

00:15:43.999 --> 00:15:49.880
The goal is consistency, and therefore the
task, while enormous, is strictly prescribed.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:52.389
That doesn’t mean it’s straightforward,
though.

00:15:52.389 --> 00:15:56.829
Every page of this document represents an
incredible amount of work—a single line

00:15:56.829 --> 00:16:02.179
might become an individual’s job for years—and
then actually putting that together, combining

00:16:02.179 --> 00:16:06.609
the tasks as small as placing the right flag
in a venue with those as large as building

00:16:06.609 --> 00:16:13.889
an entirely new stadium, is what makes for
a truly successful and impressive event.

00:16:13.889 --> 00:16:20.449
As anyone knows by now, the 2020 Tokyo Olympic
Games are actually happening in 2021.

00:16:20.449 --> 00:16:25.239
Of course, taking one of the world’s largest
events and postponing it by a year mere months

00:16:25.239 --> 00:16:29.649
before, after a decade of preparation, is
an incredible feat.

00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:33.970
I didn’t have time to cover that in this
video, but for those interested, I made a

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:37.660
companion video about the subject and put
it on Nebula.

